I can't offer a specific reason as to why your alpha is performing so
sluggishly, but I have run some benchmarks on my own alpha, if that is of
any use. If I recall correctly, the 600a has a 21164, which wasn't
especially noted for its instruction throughput per clock, just its
clockspeed as compared to the competition in its day.

API UP1000, 1x21264 600MHz, 2MB cache, 256MB RAM, Debian Linux 2.4.20

Respectively, for gcc-3.3, ccc-629 and ccc-629 with additional flags -O4
-fast -arch=ev67:

Run times: T_sequential = 459 s; T_parallel = 454 s
Run times: T_sequential = 259 s; T_parallel = 258 s
Run times: T_sequential = 232 s; T_parallel = 231 s

If you're not already aware, ccc is just a port of the Tru64 (compaq)cc to
Linux. You might also try running some additional benchmarks, to see for
yourself if the trend you observed with the atlc is mirrored with other
programs. One, albeit dated suite that is distributed as source is nbench
(do  tell  if you know of anything better).

Alex.

